\exfigure{566_q2_1}

For what values of $x$ is $x^2+x-12>0$?

solutions.latex:
\exfigure{382_q10_s1}

The vertex $(3,-4)$ is on the graph, so $f(3)=-4$, and therefore $f(x)+4=0$ has the real root $x=3$.

The other options need not be true. Take $f(x)=2(x-3)^2-4$: then $f(x)=0$ has roots $3\pm\sqrt{2}$ and $f(x)-3=0$ has roots $3\pm\sqrt{\dfrac{7}{2}}$ (neither is an integer or rational). If the parabola opens downwards, its maximum value $-4$ is greater than $-5$, so $f(x)+5=0$ has real roots.
